WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.800
So quarantine fatigue is it's a real thing.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:08.250
And when I talk about quarantine fatigue,
I'm not talking about the furious, gun toting

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:14.000
protesters that are demanding states be haphazardly
open back up, even when it's clear that states

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.790
can't be opened back up.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:20.880
When I talk about quarantine fatigue like
an adult, I'm talking to the other adults

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.500
who have been doing what the public health
officials are telling us we have to do and

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.609
who may be experiencing different types of
quarantine fatigue.

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:31.250
This can be psychological.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:36.110
This can be physical for people who are just
around less, moving around less.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.060
And it can, of course, be economic.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.290
Which we've been talking about as well.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:45.080
The people in the Corona virus is a hoax.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.730
This is government tyranny camp.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.360
They're living in another world.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.250
That's not who I'm trying to engage with here.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.730
But I do think that it's completely reasonable
that by this point, if you're in one of the

00:00:54.730 --> 00:01:01.302
states that is eight weeks into stay at home
orders, even even longer or close to it, in

00:01:01.302 --> 00:01:08.430
any case, there is significant fatigue that
comes with self-isolation.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:13.570
And the guidelines that we've been following
and I want to talk about a few different things.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.140
One is, of course, what we're doing is to
flatten the curve.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.690
And we understand that nobody's questioning
that piece.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.860
And that's why we're doing it.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.930
When we go out, we have to wear masks indoors.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:30.300
And in places where you can't stay six feet
away from people, you're not supposed to see

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.370
friends and family that you don't live with
in many professions.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:38.440
You're either working from home if you're
lucky, or you could be laid off or even permanently

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.440
fired.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.100
Your business may have closed for good, but
people can only do it at 100 percent for so

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.100
long.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.710
And the question is becoming, as people become
more fatigued, what should be done?

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:54.130
Clearly flipping a switch and going back to
the way things were would be a disaster.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.440
And we would undo a lot of the progress that
we've actually made in the last four, six,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.460
eight or even nine weeks that this has been
going on.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:08.030
But maintaining things exactly as they have
been in the most closed states is starting

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:09.950
to approach an expiration date.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:10.950
So what should be done?

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.890
There is a really good piece in the Atlantic
which explains if you just insist on keeping

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.950
everything as is keeping the strictest quarantine.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.510
It's sort of like abstinence only sex education,
which we all know doesn't work.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.510
It's not realistic.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.760
It does not actually work.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.280
The more reasonable thing would be to be clear
with people and have better, more detailed

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.150
guidelines about what people can do.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:40.910
So let me talk about the all along stuff and
then the moving forward all along.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:44.580
It would have been better to get federal guidance
on things that you should do.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.660
For example, go outside for 15 minutes every
day.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:52.450
A lot of people are not leet, not even stepping
out of the door, even if it's your front step

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:56.860
or your building's roof or a backyard, you
know, depending whether you're urban, rural,

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.380
whatever, just about everybody can step outside
for 15 minutes.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.530
And every week we're learning more about the
connection between vitamin D and Corona virus.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.840
We don't know that it's causal.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.170
We see a correlation right now, but it certainly
won't help.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.260
I'm sorry.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.890
It certainly won't hurt.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:17.700
If everybody gets 15 minutes of sun every
day, total vitamin D would be up, fewer people

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.910
would be deficient.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:23.070
Mood improves with sunlight and outdoors and
risk is very low.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.830
If you can't step outside without being six
feet away from people, then you've got to

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:28.830
wear a mask.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:29.830
Great.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.959
Would've been great to have clarity on that
earlier.

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:36.390
Most of the transmission is happening indoors
between people living together.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:40.370
Then you have transmission at indoor workplaces
and health facilities.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.730
Then you have transmission at indoor essential
businesses.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.890
Right, like grocery stores, etc.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.920
Very far down as transmission from people
outdoors.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:53.700
Of course, if people are responsibly crowd
together on a narrow hiking trail, it's bad.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.740
But the transmission happening outside from
people passing each other on bikes, this type

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:01.640
of stuff that that's been spreading as if
it's extremely dangerous.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.370
It's not zero, but it is extraordinarily low.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:10.250
And think of the good of people getting outside,
getting sunshine and hopefully doing some

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.599
exercise, walking around, etc..

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:16.350
Federal and state guidance on what are considered
low risk versus high risk activities would

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.350
be great.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:22.210
For example, and New York is now doing this
tennis versus basketball, I think I don't

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.650
have to explain why there's a dramatic difference
there.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:31.559
Also harm reduction tactics for people who
are determined that they are just going to

00:04:31.559 --> 00:04:33.189
do high risk activities.

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:38.189
We are seeing that a lot of people just don't
listen to the guidelines in any other public

00:04:38.189 --> 00:04:39.189
health venue.

00:04:39.189 --> 00:04:43.400
We would provide harm reduction if you are
going to inject heroin.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.289
We at least want to make sure you have a clean
needle so you're not spreading HIV if you

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:49.500
are going to be sexually active.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:54.400
We prefer you do it safely and give people
access to contraceptives and sex education.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.169
If you are going to absolutely be doing an
activity like this or that.

00:04:59.169 --> 00:05:02.210
Here are the things you absolutely must do
to reduce the risk.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:03.960
Now moving forward.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.780
There are governors that are managing the
psychological component of this better than

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:08.780
others.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:09.780
I'll give you an example.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.180
Is New York ready to open?

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.600
Of course not.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.129
The numbers are dramatically improved, but
the answer is they're not ready yet.

00:05:16.129 --> 00:05:17.490
So what is Andrew Cuomo doing?

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:18.490
The governor there?

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:23.039
He announced three advancements, so to speak,
yesterday.

00:05:23.039 --> 00:05:24.039
Number one.

00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:25.650
So this is all starting Friday.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.889
Landscaping and gardening businesses will
be allowed to open.

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:32.280
These are businesses where workers don't even
have to interact with customers.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.400
It's outdoors.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.400
This makes sense.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.879
And it is businesses that get to open, some
people that get to go to work.

00:05:38.879 --> 00:05:40.610
That's a good thing.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:46.479
Outdoor low risk recreational activities like
tennis, again, being able to play tennis outdoors,

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:47.479
but not basketball.

00:05:47.479 --> 00:05:48.599
It makes sense.

00:05:48.599 --> 00:05:50.509
Everyone's really close together in basketball.

00:05:50.509 --> 00:05:52.240
Not so in tennis.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.130
And Andrew Carlo is going to open or allowed
to open drive in movie theaters, which, of

00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:59.629
course, there aren't that many.

00:05:59.629 --> 00:06:02.810
I don't think there's any in New York City,
but certainly a lot of New York state, most

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.050
most of New York state is relatively rural.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.990
And I don't know the number of Drive-In theaters,
but it's an activity and it is something that

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:12.069
people can do.

00:06:12.069 --> 00:06:16.619
This is the type of thing that should be done
now because it adds almost no risk, but it

00:06:16.619 --> 00:06:20.430
builds capital because people feel like something
is going on.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:25.719
Now, it only works if people do the things
they need to do when they are doing these

00:06:25.719 --> 00:06:26.719
different things.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:31.389
Charlie Baker here in Massachusetts yesterday
announced that they're going to announce some

00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:33.669
first phase openings.

00:06:33.669 --> 00:06:35.930
That could happen as soon as next week.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.110
I assume it will be very minor, low risk stuff.

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:42.139
But again, the psychological benefit will
be very big.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:45.039
Blue states are doing better at this than
red states.

00:06:45.039 --> 00:06:51.559
In total, red states have been either doing
the stay at home order where people are rebelling

00:06:51.559 --> 00:06:54.889
and it's imploding or doing nothing.

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:58.339
And the virus is spreading like wildfire,
that there actually is a middle ground here.

00:06:58.339 --> 00:07:04.110
It involves very low risk activities, harm
reduction, making people aware that there

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:07.469
are things they could and even should be doing
that are going to be good for their mental

00:07:07.469 --> 00:07:12.800
health and their physical health and get you
more buy in and allow people to behave safely

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.129
for a longer period of time.

00:07:15.129 --> 00:07:18.289
Hopefully we'll see more governors do these
types of things

